{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/902420-vampire-narratives-syllabus-as-requested-by-some","id":902420,"title":"Vampire Narratives Syllabus, as requested by some","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-05-28T18:36:03-07:00","id":902420,"title":"Vampire Narratives Syllabus, as requested by some","content":"I\u2019ve mentioned a few times that I took an intellectual and cultural history class called Vampire Narratives as an undergrad, and a few people have asked for more information. I\u2019ve typed out the syllabus below, for anyone who wants to see what texts we used and how we used them. This was not written by me, it was written by Professor Katharina Vester at American University. I\u2019ve cut out sections on grading, course format, academic support, and campus resources.<br /> <br /> <b>AMST 296-001/HIST 296-003</b><br /> <b>Blood and Desire: Vampire Narratives and American Society</b><br /> For almost two hundred years, vampire narratives have served as projection surfaces for cultural angst and fears of social change. This course investigates how vampires have been used to negotiate immigration and xenophobia, women\u2019s suffrage, fear of miscegenation, the Cold War, homophobia, and AIDS. The course considers what aims the contemporary obsession with vampire narratives serves and what it reveals about today\u2019s culture.<br /> <b>The material discussed in this class, because of the nature of the genre, is often gruesome and at times sexually explicit. As you decide to take this class, please consider that watching and reading the materials are crucial to your success in class but not all of this material may be pleasant to read.</b><br /> [\u2026]<br /> <b>Learning Objectives:</b><ul><li>To understand the recent vampire craze within its historical context.</li> <li>To investigate how the change in vampire narratives reflects the cultural context of the time.</li> <li>To access US history through popular culture.</li> <li>To understand how popular culture promotes and resists gender, race, and class ideology.</li> </ul>[\u2026]<br /> <b>08/26 Introduction: Why Vampires? </b><br /> <br /> <b>08/29 Each Era Has Its Own Vampire</b><br /> Read: Eveline Brugger: \u201cWhere Do the Cullens Fit In? Vampires in European Folklore, Science and Fiction;\u201d Nina Auerbach from <i>Our Vampires/Ourselves: </i>\u201cIntroduction,\u201d \u201cByron\u2019s Ghost.\u201d<br /> <br /> <b><i>Part 1: </i></b><b><i>The Vampire in Western Literature and History</i></b><br /> <br /> <b>09/02 Bloody Beginnings</b><br /> Read: John Polidori, <i>The Vampyre </i>(free on the Internet. For instance: <a href=\"http://www.sff.net/people/\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.sff.net/people/</a> doylemacdonald/l_vampyr.htm.)<br /> <br /> <b>09/05 Carmilla: The Rise of the Female Vampire</b><br /> Read: J. Sheridan LeFanu, <i>Carmilla </i>(free on the Internet. For instance: <a href=\"http://www.sff.net/people/\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.sff.net/people/</a> doylemacdonald/l_carmil.htm.)<br /> <br /> <b>09/09 The Original <i>Dracula</i></b><br /> Read: Bram Stoker, <i>Dracula</i> (chapters 1-5, 7, 8, 10, 12, 13, 16, 18, 19, 21) (free on the Internet. For instance: <a href=\"http://www.literature.org/authors/stoker-bram/dracula/\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.literature.org/authors/stoker-bram/dracula/</a>.)<br /> <br /> <b>09/12 Bram Stoker\u2019s <i>Dracula--</i>Criticism</b><br /> Read: Franco Moretti: \u201cA Capital Dracula\u201d; Jimmie Cain: \u201cRacism and the Vampire: The Anti-Slavic Premise of Bram Stoker\u2019s <i>Dracula </i>(1897).\u201d<br /> <br /> <b><i>Part 2: The History of Vampires in Popular Culture</i></b><br /> <br /> <b>09/16 Silent Screams: Dracula on the Silver Screen</b><br /> Watch: <i>Dracula</i><br /> Read: Nina Auerbach; \u201cDracula and Draculas\u201d; start Richard Matheson, <i>I Am Legend.</i><br /> <br /> <b>09/19 World War II: Vampires and War Propaganda</b><br /> Watch: <i>The Return of the Vampire</i><br /> Read: Richard Matheson, <i>I Am Legend</i><br /> <br /> <b>09/23 Cold War Vampire Movies</b><br /> Watch: <i>Horror of Dracula</i><br /> Read: Lois H. Palmer: \u201cHammering it Home\u201d; Richard Matheson, <i>I Am Legend.</i><br /> <br /> <b>09/26 Fear of Mass Destruction: Vampires and the Cold War</b><br /> Read: Finish Richard Matheson, <i>I Am Legend </i>for today\u2019s meeting; start Anne Rice, <i>Interview with the Vampire.</i><br /> <br /> <b>09/30 <i>I Am Legend</i>: Interpretations</b><br /> Watch: <i>Omega Man; I Am Legend </i><br /> Read: Anne Rice, <i>Interview with the Vampire.</i><br /> <br /> <b>10/03 1970s: Vampires and Blaxploitation </b><br /> Watch: <i>Blacula</i><br /> Read: Paul Lehman and John Edgar Browning: \u201cThe <i>Dracula</i> and the <i>Blacula </i>(1972) Cultural Revolution;\u201d Anne Rice, <i>Interview with the Vampire.</i><br /> <br /> <b>10/07 The 1970s Vampire Revival: <i>Interview with the Vampire</i></b><br /> Read: Finish Anne Rice, <i>Interview with the Vampire </i>for today\u2019s meeting.<br /> <br /> <b>10/10 Reagan\u2019s Vampires: Consumerism and the Family </b><br /> Watch: <i>The Lost Boys</i><br /> Read: Nicola Nixon: \u201cWhen Hollywood Sucks, or, Hungry Girls, Lost Boys, and Vampirism in the Age of Reagan\u201d; start with Stephenie Meyer,<i> Twilight</i>.<br /> <br /> <b>10/14 AIDS and the Vampire Film</b><br /> <b>Quiz 2</b><br /> Watch: <i>Bram Stoker\u2019s Dracula</i><br /> Read: Lisa Nystrom: \u201cBlood, Lust, and the Fe/Male Narrative in <i>Bram Stoker\u2019s Dracula</i> (1992) and the Novel (1897)\u201d; Stephenie Meyer,<i> Twilight</i>.<br /> <br /> <b>10/17 Black Masculinity and the <i>Blade</i> Trilogy</b><br /> Watch: <i>Blade</i><br /> Read: Gretchen Bakke: \u201cDead White Men: An Essay on the Changing Dynamics of Race in US Action Cinema\u201d; Stephenie Meyer,<i> Twilight</i>.<br /> <br /> <b><i>Part 3: Case Studies</i></b><br /> <br /> <b>10/21 <i>Buffy, the Vampire Slayer</i>: The Allure</b><br /> To do: If you haven\u2019t watched <i>Buffy, the Vampire Slayer</i> before, you may want to do some online-research to help you understand the structure and content of the series. Wikipedia offers an excellent plot summary of all seven seasons. On Blackboard you will find Roz Kaveney\u2019s \u201cShe Saved the World. A Lot\u201d which provides an extensive introduction to the series.<br /> Watch: <i>Buffy, the Vampire Slayer, </i>season 4: episodes \u201cFreshman\u201d and \u201cLiving Conditions.\u201d<br /> Read: Patricia Pender: \u201c\u2019I am Buffy, and\u2026You\u2019re History:\u2019 The Postmodern Politics of Buffy\u201d; Stephenie Meyer,<i> Twilight</i>.<br /> <br /> <b>10/24 <i>Buffy, the Vampire Slayer</i>: Femininity and Feminism </b><br /> Watch: <i>Buffy, the Vampire Slayer, </i>season 2, episode \u201cHalloween\u201d; and season 5, episode \u201cBuffy vs. Dracula.\u201d<br /> Read: Mimi Marinucci: \u201cFemininsm and the Ethics of Violence: Why Buffy Kicks Ass;\u201d Elyce Rae Helford: \u201c\u2019My Emotions Give me Power:\u2019 The Containment of Girls\u2019 Anger in Buffy\u201d; Stephenie Meyer,<i> Twilight</i>.<br /> <br /> <b>10/28 <i>Buffy, the Vampire Slayer</i>: Race and Aesthetic Innovation</b><br /> Watch: <i>Buffy, the Vampire Slayer, </i>season 6, episode \u201cOnce More (With Feeling),\u201d season 4, episode \u201cHush.\u201d<br /> Read: Kent Ono: \u201cTo be a Vampire on <i>Buffy, the Vampire Slayer</i>. Race and (\u2018Other\u2019) Socially Marginalizing Positions on Horror TV\u201d; Stephenie Meyer,<i> Twilight</i>.<br /> <br /> <b>10/31 <i>Twilight</i>: Gender and the American Dream</b><br /> Read: Melissa Ames: \u201c<i>Twilight</i> Follows Tradition: Analyzing Biting Critiques of Vampire Narratives for Their Portrayal of Gender and Sexuality\u201d; Sara Buttsworth, \u201c\u2019CinderBella\u2019: <i>Twilight</i>, Fairy Tales, and the Twenty-First Century American Dream.\u201d Finish Stephenie Meyer\u2019s<i> Twilight</i> for today\u2019s meeting.<br /> <br /> <b>11/04 <i>Twilight</i> and the Body</b><br /> Read: Karin Nykvist: \u201cThe Body Project\u201d; Sara K\u00e4rrholm, \u201c\u2019Loving You is Like Loving the Dead\u2019: Eroticization of the Dead Body\u201d; Mariah Larson: \u201c\u2019I know what I saw:\u2019 The Female Gaze and the Male Object of Desire.\u201d <br /> <br /> <b>11/07 <i>Twilight:</i> Race and Ethnicity</b><br /> Watch: <i>The Twilight Saga: New Moon</i><br /> Read: Natalie Wilson, \u201cCivilized Vampires Versus Savage Werewolves: Race and Ethnicity in the Twilight Series.\u201d<br /> <br /> <b>11/11 <i>Twilight:</i> Fan Culture</b><br /> Read: Elizabeth Behm-Morawitz, Melissa A. Click, &amp; Jennifer Stevens Aubrey, \u201cRelating to Twilight: Fans\u2019 Responses to Love and Romance in the Vampire Franchise\u201d; Jessica Sheffield &amp; Elyse Merlo, \u201cBiting Back: Twilight Anti-Fandom and the Rhetoric of Superiority.\u201d<br /> <br /> <b>11/14 <i>True Blood</i>: The South</b><br /> If you haven\u2019t watched <i>True Blood</i> before, you may want to engage in some research. The HBO homepage offers some previews and information. More information you can find on IMDb and Wikipedia. You may also want to check out TrueBlood.net.<br /> Watch: Season 1, episode \u201cStrange Love.\u201d<br /> Read: Evangelia Kindinger, \u201cReading Supernatural Fiction as Regional Fiction\u201d; Caroline Ruddell and Brigid Cherry, \u201c\u2019More Than Cold and Heartless:\u2019 The Southern Gothic Milieu of True Blood\u201d; Victoria Amador: \u201cBlacks and Whites: Trash and Good Country People in True Blood.\u201d<br /> <br /> <b>11/18 <i>True Blood</i>: Sexuality</b><br /> Watch: Season 1, episode \u201cMine!\u201d<br /> Read: Patricia Brace and Robert Arp: \u201cComing out of the Coffin and Coming out of the Closet\u201d; Darren Elliot-Smith, \u201cThe Homosexual Vampire is a Metaphor for\u2026The Homosexual Vampire?\u201d<br /> <br /> <br /> Again, I did not compose this syllabus, this was written by Professor Vester at American University.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:12px\">-</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":11}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3093-hm8453a","name":"hm8453a","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3093_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"636"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-22T20:28:31-07:00","id":956111,"title":"","content":"BUMPing, in case there's more interest.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:12px\">-</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3093-hm8453a","name":"hm8453a","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3093_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"636"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-22T21:17:04-07:00","id":956141,"title":"","content":"I took a similar course in undergrad. This was the syllabus for the course (with the academic book keeping removed). One will probably notice a decent bit of overlap, but it also covers some other bases as well. Disclaimer: I did not compose this syllabus (though any typographical or grammatical errors are mine). It was written by Dr. Lisa Di Bartolomeo of West Virginia University.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>FLIT 276</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>Vampire: Blood and Revolution</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>Course Description</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">This course examines the phenomenon of vampirism in verbal and visual culture from different periods in various cultures (Bulgaria, Czechoslovakia, Romania, Russia, Serbia, France, England, America, Africa and the Caribbean). Why do vampires capture the imagination, especially of Anglophone readers and viewers? What qualities does the vampire incarnate? Which historical events or customs have triggered particular enthusiasm for depicting the undead? How has the depiction of the vampire evolved over centuries? Our discussions will address these and related issues as we analyze stories, novels, films, legends, fairy tales, television shows, and historical studies, focusing on vampires from a variety of critical perspectives and contextualizing the works in the cultures that produced them.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>Expected Learning Outcomes</b></span></span><ol class=\"decimal\"><li><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Investigate the ethnographic and folkloric origins of the vampire legends arising in Eastern and Central Europe;</span></span></li> <li><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Interpret and analyze a literary or filmic work based on artistic merit, narrative coherence, and social commentary;</span></span></li> <li><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Critique the ideological, political, and social-cultural bias of a text or film;</span></span></li> <li><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Analyze films and literary texts from a variety of critical perspectives, such as feminist, Marxist, psychoanalytic, and historical-medical;</span></span></li> <li><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Determine the relevance of various texts for today\u2019s society;</span></span></li> <li><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Interrogate a given culture\u2019s expected norms and discern the threat posed by outsiders such as vampires;</span></span></li> <li><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Discern in modern interpretations of vampire tales the concerns and problems facing society at various points in history;</span></span></li> <li><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Evaluate the need for an outside figure on whom to focus blame and direct animosity;</span></span></li> <li><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Understand the obsession with vampires the world over, as expressed in visual and verbal culture;</span></span></li> </ol><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>Required Texts</b></span></span><ol class=\"decimal\"><li><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Raymond T. McNally &amp; Radu Florescu. </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>In Search of Dracula. The History of Dracula and Vampires.</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Boston/NY: Houghton Mifflin Co., 1992/1994.</span></span></li> <li><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Alan Ryan. </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>The Penguin Book of Vampire Stories</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">. NY: Penguin Books, 1988.</span></span></li> <li><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Nina Auerbach &amp; David J. Skal, eds. </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Dracula: Bram Stoker</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">. NY/London: W.W. Norton &amp; Co., 1997.</span></span></li> <li><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Anne Rice. </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Interview with the Vampire.</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> NY: Ballantine Books, 1997.</span></span></li> <li><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Charlaine Harris. </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Dead Until Dark</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">. NY: Ace Books, 2001.</span></span></li> <li><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Course pack, available on ereserves.</span></span></li> </ol><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">The designation (D) refers to the Norton Critical Edition of </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Dracula</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">, which, apart from the text of Stoker\u2019s novel, contains critical essays (#3 above).</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">(AR) references the collection of stories edited by Alan Ryan (#2 above).</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">The notation (CP) refers to Xeroxes either distributed in class or contained in the course pack on ereserves (#6 above).</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Daily Schedule</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Week One. August.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">T., 19.08. Introduction: distribution of syllabi, practical matters. SIGNS OF THE VAMPIRE: accoutrements, setting, activities, prophylactics. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">*HW: (to be completed for Thursday): Read: folktales \u201cThe Vampire\u201d and \u201cThe Soldier and the Vampire\u201d (CP); recorded legends Jan Perkowski (CP); Felix J. Oinas, \u201cEast European Vampires\u201d (CP); McNally and Florescu, pp. 117-32, and 193-219.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">TH.,21.08. Significance and diversity of APPROACHES: historical, political, social, religious, psychological/sexual, medical, metaphorical, synthetic. Folktales, legends, myths in Eastern and Central Europe. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">*HW: Read: McNally &amp; Florescu, pp. 15-42, and 60-103.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Week Two</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica, Arial, Verdana, sans-serif\">T., 26.08. HISTORICAL basis: Vlad Tepes &amp; Erszebet (Elizabeth) Bathory. Clips of Dark Prince (2000).</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">*HW: Read: John Polidori, \u201cThe Vampyre\u201d (1819) (AR=Alan Ryan collection, pp. 7-24); Thomas Ligotti, \u201cThe Lost Art of Twilight\u201d (1987) (CP); Chelsea Quinn Yarbo, \u201cCabin 33\u201d (1980) (AR 451-504).</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Th., 28.08. Gothic and (neo-) Romantic period: spirituality, search for the ineffable, immortality through art, maximal individualism (polarization), Satanism. Vampire as Aristocrat and artist/aesthete. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">*HW: Read: Bram Stoker, </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Dracula</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> (1897) (D): pp. 9-132.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Week Three. September</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">T., 2.09. MODERN VAMPIRE: Dracula on page and screen. Begin discussion of</span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i> Dracula.</i></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">*HW: Read: Christopher Craft, \u201c\u2019Kiss Me with Those Ruby Lips\u2019: Gender and Inversion in Bram Stoker\u2019s </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Dracula</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">\u201d (D): 444-59; and Stoker, </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Dracula</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> (D): 133-240.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Th., 4.09. STOKER\u2019S DRACULA. Empire and alterity, patriarchal male bonding; female as \u201cweak vessel,\u201d object of rescue. If women are penetrated by an alien force to which they submit, are men next in line? Medical and legal men as authorities. Invaded borders and empire. \u201cPossession.\u201d The New Woman (Mina\u2019s shorthand) in Stoker\u2019s misogynistic framework. Victorian science. Theories of evolution (Darwin); colonization. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">*HW: Read Stoker, </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Dracula</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">, pp. 241-327, and Phyllis A. Roth, \u201cSuddenly Sexual Women in Bram Stoker\u2019s </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Dracula</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">\u201d (D): 411-21.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Week Four</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">T., 9.09. STOKER\u2019S DRACULA. Finish discussion of novel. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">*HW: Read: Carol A. Senf, \u201cDracula: The Unseen Face in the Mirror\u201d (D): 421-31; Stephen D. Arata, \u201cThe Occidental Tourist: </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Dracula</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> and the Anxiety of Reverse Colonization\u201d (D): 462-70; Franco Moretti, \u201c[A Capital </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Dracula</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">]\u201d (D): 431-44.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Th., 11.09. DRACULA critiques. Discussion of critical articles. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">*HW: Read: Bram Dijkstra, \u201cDracula\u2019s Backlash\u201d (D): 460-62; </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Week Five</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">T., 16.09. NOSFERATU. F.W. Murnau, </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Nosferatu: A Symphony of Horror</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> (1922), with Max Shreck (68 minutes, in class). Alterity of a stereotypical Jewish appearance\u2014visual impact; forces of light versus darkness (rodents, disease, etc.) in German Expressionist mode.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Th., 18.09. NOSFERATU. Finish watching and discuss Murnau\u2019s film. Watch clips of Werner Herzog, </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Nosferatu the Vampyre</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> (1979), with Klaus Kinski. Polarization of aesthetics: the frisson of \u201cbeauty and the beast\u201d contrast as part of decadence and disease, rodents and plague (evolutionary scale). The tragic vampire, parallelism of opposites. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Week Six</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Th., 25.09. Tod Browning, </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Dracula </i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">(1931), with Bela Lugosi\u2014video (75 minutes) in class. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Signaling alterity through sound: foreign accent as non-assimilation.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">*HW: Read: Nina Auerbach, \u201cVampires in the Light\u201d (D): 389-404. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Week Seven. October.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">T., 30.09. Hammar Studio Draculas and Christopher Lee. Stage Draculas Bela Lugosi and Frank Langella. Discussion of Lugosi\u2019s characterization, compared with Lee (shown in clips in class), in Terence Fisher\u2019s </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Dracula/Horror of Dracula</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> (1958). More explicit eroticization: Drac as mesmerizing icon. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">*HW: View John Badham, </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Dracula</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> (1979), with Frank Langella\u2014video (109 minutes).</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Th., 2.10. John Badham, </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Dracula</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> (1979), with Frank Langella. Romantic sexualization through body type, gesture, attire, style, baby. The melancholy, tragic vampire in an era of feminism. Discussion of all three (1931, 1958, 1979) embodiments of Stoker\u2019s Count. What distinguishes one Drac from the next? What links them? </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">*HW: Watch Coppola\u2019s </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Bram Stoker\u2019s Dracula.</i></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Week Eight</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">T., 7.10. Discuss Francis Ford Coppola\u2019s 1992 film, </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Bram Stoker\u2019s Dracula</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">*HW: Read in CP: Diane DeKelb-Rittenhouse, \u201cSex and the Single Vampire: The Evolution of the Vampire Lothario and Its Representation in </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Buffy,</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">\u201d (CP): 143-152; Zoe-Jane Playden, \u201cWhat You Are, What\u2019s to Come: Feminism, Citizenship and the Divine,\u201d (CP): 120-147.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Th., 9.10. BUFFY-LICIOUS. View in class \u201cBuffy versus Dracula.\u201d </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Buffy the Vampire Slayer</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">: Female empowerment, female heroism, demons as psychological issues made manifest.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Week Nine</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">T., 14.10. View clips of </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Blacula</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> (1972, William Crain) and discuss. Blaxploitation meets Vampirism. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">*HW: Read Alan Ryan, \u201cFollowing the Way\u201d (AR 562-73).</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Th., 16.10. Vampirism as religion. Discuss \u201cFollowing the Way.\u201d Watch \u201cBuffy the Vampire Slayer:\u201d \u201cLie to Me.\u201d Discuss vamps as cult objects. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">*HW: Read: Bram Stoker, \u201cDracula\u2019s Guest\u201d (1914) (AR 163-74, also in (D); \u201cC.L. Moore, \u201cShambleau\u201d (1933) (AR 255-81). </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Week Ten</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">T., 21.10. Female Vampirism. Woman as figure of sexual power, set up to be destroyed.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Th., 23.10. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">*HW: Read: J. Sheridan Le Fanu, \u201cCarmilla\u201d (1871-72) (AR 71-137), Andrea Weiss, \u201cThe Vampire Lovers\u201d (1992) (CP). </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Week Eleven</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">T., 28.10. Lesbian Vampirism. Discuss \u201cCarmilla.\u201d Watch clips of Roy Ward Baker, </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>The Vampire Lovers</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> (1970/1): an aesthetics on loan from male pornography; the male delectation of semi-dressed \u201cfemininity\u201d versus the hero as representative of safe and \u201cnormal\u201d status quo. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">*HW: View Tony Scott\u2019s, </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>The Hunger</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> (1983).</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Th., 30.10. Happy (almost) Halloween! Lesbian Vampirism. Discuss </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>The </i></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Hunger</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">: recasting oppositions: hot versus cold; dark versus light; </span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">sound versus sight. Music as thematic organizer (style versus mode of </span></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">life). Darwinism, power play, AIDS.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">*HW: Anne Rice, </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Interview with the Vampire</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> (1976): up to p. 168.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Th., 6.11. Vampire Revival in the Late 20</span><sup><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">th</span></sup><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Century. Subjectivity, homoeroticism, AIDS. Vampire as existential seeker; identity crisis; family and communal imperatives; womanhood and maturity. Clips from </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Angel</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">*HW: Finish reading Rice, </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Interview with the Vampire</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Week Thirteen</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">T., 11.11. Finish discussion of </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Interview</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">*HW: Watch Neil Jordan, </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Interview with the Vampire</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> (1994) also, read Sandra Tomc, \u201cDieting and Damnation: Anne Rice\u2019s </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Interview with the Vampire</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">,\u201d (from </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Blood Read</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">, eds. Joan Gordon &amp; Veronica Hollinger [Philadelphia: University of Pennsylvania Press, 1997]), pp. 95-113 (CP).</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Th., 13.11. Discussion of Jordan\u2019s film version of </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Interview.</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Clips from </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Angel</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">*HW: Watch </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Blade.</i></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Week Fourteen</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">T., 18.11. Discuss </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Blade </i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">(1998, Stephen Norrington)</span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>.</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Also, vampires in reggae music: white man as exploiter/vampire. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">*HW: Watch </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>The Lost Boys</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> (1987, Joel Schumacher) at home, or at the screening (Room 104 the basement of Wise Library, from 5:30-7:30, Tuesday, 18.11); also, read in CP Nicola Nixon, \u201cWhen Hollywood Sucks, or, Hungry Girls, Lost Boys, and Vampirism in the Age of Reagan\u201d (115-128), and Rob Latham, \u201cConsuming Youth: The Lost Boys Cruise Mallworld\u201d (129-147).</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Th., 20.11. Discuss </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>The Lost Boys.</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Hip bloodsuckers in Peter Pan mode\u2014only in California! View clips from </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Near Dark</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> (1987, Kathryn Bigelow). </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">*HW: Watch </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Underworld.</i></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Week Fifteen. December. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">T., 2.12. Dark Comics: </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Underworld</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> (2003, Len Wiseman). How true to the myth of vampirism is this saga? How can Kate kick so much butt in skin-tight leather? Why does she need a flashlight? </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">*HW: Read Charlaine Harris, </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><i>Dead Until Dark.</i></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Th., 4.12 . Discuss Charlaine Harris, <i>Dead Until Dark.</i> What new issues does this book bring to vampires? How does Harris change the rules? What sort of vamp do we have with Bill? Summary of course issues. Questions. </span></span><br /> <br /> <br /> To reiterate the disclaimer, I do not own this syllabus. It was written by Dr. Lisa Di Bartolomeo of West Virginia Univeristy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2507,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","user":"Caitiff Primogen","date":". 08-23-2016, 10:52 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-23T23:09:38-07:00","id":956806,"title":"","content":"All this, and not a single section or mention of the 90's phenomenon that was Masquerade?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/231-blitzburger","name":"Blitzburger","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar231_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"183"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-24T06:15:13-07:00","id":956902,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blitzburger</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/902420-vampire-narratives-syllabus-as-requested-by-some?p=956806#post956806\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">All this, and not a single section or mention of the 90's phenomenon that was Masquerade?</div> </div> </div> </div>I know! I yelled at Vester about it (I almost TAd another session of the class, and we were discussing the new syllabus).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:12px\">-</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3093-hm8453a","name":"hm8453a","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3093_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"636"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-24T08:15:25-07:00","id":956977,"title":"","content":"I was trying to convince my girlfriend to take a vampire course as an elective this past year, but alas her schedule was full with poking brains and such-like. Can't grab a syllabus but the course title was &quot;The Vampire: Modernity and the Undead&quot;. Interestingly, you could count it towards a Minor in &quot;<span style=\"color:#52514F\"><span style=\"font-family:ClassicGrotesque\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Esoteric and Occult Traditions&quot; which sounds like a right party. </span></span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2291-gwyn-chan-r-gwyll","name":"Gwyn chan 'r Gwyll","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-24T08:17:40-07:00","id":956979,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gwyn chan 'r Gwyll</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/902420-vampire-narratives-syllabus-as-requested-by-some?p=956977#post956977\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was trying to convince my girlfriend to take a vampire course as an elective this past year, but alas her schedule was full with poking brains and such-like. Can't grab a syllabus but the course title was &quot;The Vampire: Modernity and the Undead&quot;. Interestingly, you could count it towards a Minor in &quot;<span style=\"color:#52514F\"><span style=\"font-family:ClassicGrotesque\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Esoteric and Occult Traditions&quot; which sounds like a right party. </span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div>So cool! Ours was a history course.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:12px\">-</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3093-hm8453a","name":"hm8453a","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3093_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"636"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-24T08:24:23-07:00","id":956983,"title":"","content":"This one was primarily in Early Modern / Contemporary Studies. Whatever that means. I guess history mixed with basic sociology and cultural studies? Even less well-defined than my field of &quot;Middle Eastern Studies&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2291-gwyn-chan-r-gwyll","name":"Gwyn chan 'r Gwyll","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-24T10:21:35-07:00","id":957033,"title":"","content":"Ours was considered part of Foreign Literature. I honestly can't remember what it could be counted towards as i just took it have something less crunchy in my schedule (and also learn about vampires).<br /> <br /> V:tM came up in class discussion (there were a couple other gamers in my section). The professors said it had also come up in previous years but she didn't include it in the syllabus because she wasn't sufficiently familiar with it to have an informed academic opinion on it, as well as not having much room to add it with everything else. It was part of the conversation though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-24T16:35:23-07:00","id":957261,"title":"","content":"I...I don't even know if you even can study something like this here. Best shot at something like this would be probably literature and picking these as topics of your articles.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-24T16:36:35-07:00","id":957262,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>WHW</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/902420-vampire-narratives-syllabus-as-requested-by-some?p=957261#post957261\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I...I don't even know if you even can study something like this here. Best shot at something like this would be probably literature and picking these as topics of your articles.</div> </div> </div> </div>Do you mind if I ask what school you attend?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:12px\">-</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3093-hm8453a","name":"hm8453a","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3093_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"636"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-24T16:43:01-07:00","id":957265,"title":"","content":"Adam's Mickiewicz University in Pozna\u0144. By &quot;here&quot; I meant the whole country in general. But I guess that's the freedom of being able to pick your courses.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-24T21:01:05-07:00","id":957384,"title":"","content":"As Hayley already knows, I once wrote a english paper on the similarities between Hemingway's work and Gen X pop culture that touched on what vampires symbolized to gen X<br /> <br /> Here is that section of the paper<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Of course the slacker wasn't the only figure Gen X came up with to share Krebs' role as a representative of a conservative society\u2019s outcasts. While most people would associate the use of the vampire as a sympathetic figure with the millennial generation <i>(Twilight </i>being the obvious example), the trend of using vampires as a sympathetic representation of society\u2019s outcasts began with Generation X. The trend of the vampire as an outcast from a conservative society has been around since Dracula in the Victorian era (paraphrase<span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"> of Davis 1)</span></span>. But it was in the 80s that the trend resurfaced in modern pop culture.<br /> Part of the reason the trend resurfaced, according to S Peter Davis in his article <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">6</span></span><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><i> Mind-Blowing Ways Zombies and Vampires Explain America,</i></span></span><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"> is that vampires, as parasitic, sexually deviant foreigners with their own weird customs and society, make a pretty good metaphor for the conservative view of liberals (summary of Davis 1). The 80s, being a conservative decade, thus began using vampires as outcasts in it's movies. Of course, the best example of vampires as a metaphor for the wild, outcast Generation X was </span></span><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><i>The Lost Boys</i></span></span><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">, which, as the cast of </span></span><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><i>After Hours: Why everyone wants to have sex with vampires</i></span></span><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">, points out was one of the first vampire teen movies that \u201ctook the idea of wild reckless youth to it's natural conclusion, fanged nocturnal monsters\u201d (quoted in Viar</span></span><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><i> ).</i></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">Of course, as the </span></span><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><i>After Hours</i></span></span><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"> cast points out, the appeal of the vampire is based on disillusionment and the sense of being an outcast (paraphrase of Viar). These are the same traits that Krebs and the slacker are based on. In fact, the very title of </span></span><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><i>The Lost Boys</i></span></span><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"> is a reference to Peter Pan, who never had to grow up, and thus never had to take part in the same adult society that the slacker and Krebs rejected. Like the slacker, the vampire presents the idea of being an outcast as a good thing. The disillusionment, like that of the slacker and Krebs, comes from frustration with the young generation's elders, as demonstrated in the hit 90s role playing game </span></span><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><i>Vampire: the Masquerade,</i></span></span><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"> in which a prophecy that the younger vampires would be eaten by their elders at the end of the world played a major role in the game's meta plot. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">In the end, the defining traits of Nomad generations like the the Lost Generation and Generation X are disillusionment and a sense of being an outcast, traits that affected both the work of Hemingway, including </span></span><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><i>Soldier's home</i></span></span><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">, and the pop culture of Generation X</span></span> </div> </div> </div><br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Despite the user name I am actually bigender.<br /> <br /> My Savannah Setting for CofD can be found <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/959455-the-young-man-in-the-cafe-s-savannah-georgia-setting-writeup\" target=\"_blank\">here</a><br /> <br /> My heroes as monster tamers rules for BtP can be found <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/975230-heroes-as-monster-tamers-rules-ideas\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3932-the-young-man-in-the-cafe","name":"The young man in the cafe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3932_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}